<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">István Széchenyi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Count István Széchenyi was a Hungarian reformer and statesman, called 'the greatest Hungarian' by his rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a>. He funded the founding of the Hungarian Academy of Sciences in 1825, promoted steam navigation on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the regulation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, and initiated the Chain Bridge between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> and Pest. His books urged the nobility to give up its privileges, and he served in the government of 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istvan-szechenyi.jpg" alt="István Széchenyi" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miklós Barabás · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gr%C3%B3f_Sz%C3%A9chenyi_Istv%C3%A1n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
